  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 417 PM EDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nWhile all eyes are on the southeasterly digging front that could \nlikely impact the region over the weekend, we also need to look out \nfor potential impacts that will precede the front on Friday. In \nthe lower levels, moisture advection from the south-southwest at \nthe 700 and 850mb levels will drive a shallow but moist airmass \nover the region. Dewpoints values with this airmass are near the \n90th percentile for this time of year, and coupled with surface \ntemperatures in the low 90s, feels-like temperatures will reach \ninto the low 100s across the region. Light surface winds will set \nus up for a near typical diurnal-heating pattern on Friday, with \nprecipitation focused over inland South Florida. The thick low-\nlevel moisture layer will be ripe for releasing pockets of heavy \nrainfall, with ensemble guidance pointing to a 20-30% chance of 1 \ninch of rainfall and pockets of 2-4 inches in some areas. The sea \nbreeze along the eastern metro areas is forecast to keep the \nheaviest rainfall well inland as it progresses west, but a shower \nor two can not be ruled out over the metro areas. There is good \nagreement amongst the ensemble members that temperatures aloft on \nFriday are predicted to be warmer than average, which should keep \nthe risk of severe thunderstorms at marginal levels. All of this \ntogether points to the main risks for Friday being heat related, \nwith inland areas at risk for isolated pockets of heavy rainfall.\n\nMoving into the weekend, the speed of the propagating front will \nbe a big player in the amount of rainfall that we may see over the\nweekend. The Weather Prediction Center (WPC) cluster analysis \nshows that the majority of the variance can be described by two \ndifferent scenarios based on the speed of the front. The first \nscenario, which is more favored by the American ensemble (GEFS), \nshows that a more quickly propagating front will increase the \namount of rain cross South Florida compared to the grand ensemble.\nThe second scenario is a more slowly propagating front which \npoints towards a reduction in the amount of rainfall in our area \ncompared to the grand ensemble. This second scenario is mostly \nfavored by the European ensemble (ENS), while the Canadian \nensemble (GEPS) is split between the two scenarios. More concrete \nrainfall totals for this event are still murky as the speed of the\nfront is not yet fully resolved, but the WPC rainfall forecast is\nfavoring higher totals (about 2-4 inches over the weekend) over \nthe eastern metro area. We will be providing more information \nregarding impacts as the forecast becomes clearer so please stay \ntuned as we approach the weekend.\n\nFollowing the front, there is better agreement across the ensemble \nguidance that a steep pressure gradient will set up over the entire \nstate of Florida, bringing with it a jump in easterly winds. The \nexact timing of the onset of winds here will be tied to the speed \nand direction of the high pressure system building behind the front \nwhich is not completely resolved yet.",
